## Error handler does not ordinarily return

0x6cd8's normal control flow may call recovery0x63b8 at0x6e2c. Its terminal
fallthrough performs cleanup and at0x6e90 executes BL0x1fc (bytesd9e4ffeb).
0x1fc clearsr0-r12;0x230 contains EAFFFFFE, an unconditional self-loop.
The apparent POP/BX-LR epilogue at0x6e94/0x6e98 is therefore not reached by an
ordinary returning path. Neither BL nor a visible epilogue proves a soft check.
This does not assert a complete anti-rollback policy for every boot path.

The separate logger0x6bec has a genuine returning path at0x6ca8/0x6cac.
Its default implementation appends24-byte records until count reaches20,
then returns0 without appending; it does not wrap in the inspected path.
A registered hook can replace that default path, and invalid input enters
0x6cd8. The blanket label 'log-and-return' belongs neither to all error
handling nor to the0x6cd8 routine.

