### Preliminary static instruction coverage

Ran the attached Python/Capstone5.0.7 survey from59 verified symbol/vector/
callback seeds. Followed direct ARM branches/calls and literal-loaded PC targets;
stopped unresolved indirect calls/returns/computed branches. Known no-return
calls1fc/6cd8 suppress continuations. Excluded observed zero suffix, separately
copied initialization templates, data-symbol starts and discovered literal words.
A second pass stabilized literal exclusions.

Result:11989 unique aligned instruction addresses,47956 bytes (46.83KiB),
272 distinct direct-call destinations (NOT a certified function count),447
literal-word exclusions. This is neither an execution trace nor a final code/data
partition. Branch feasibility and transitive no-return behavior are not fully
analysed; there may be both false-positive classification and uncovered code.
It is stronger evidence than a blind linear disassembly/decodability count.

Independent static check reproduced the address/count outputs and checked PC-
write metadata plus code-to-pool boundaries using GNU ARM objdump. A separately
written direct-control-field walker from only40 function seeds obtained11908
addresses/47632 bytes (46.52KiB), all in the primary set. Further filtering which
stops every unconditional call continuation retained5190 addresses/20760 bytes
(20.27KiB). That is a robustness check, NOT a formal lower bound: its call-target
seeds were discovered in the initial pass permitting ordinary call returns.

Honest conclusion: tens of KiB of statically identified A32 instructions; the
whole-image4–12KiB claim is unsupported. Do not replace it with an assertion
that exactly46.83KiB of the image is code. A reviewed byte-range inventory is
still needed, especially across unresolved indirect transfers.

